dict(pas="9.1", w="子/the Master | 罕=hǎn/seldom | 言/spoke of | 利/profit | 與=yǔ/and | 命/the decree (of Heaven) | 與=yǔ/and | 仁。/humaneness",
  l="The Master seldom spoke of profit, and the decree, and humaneness.",
  i="Profit, what Heaven ordains, and humaneness: these the Master seldom talked about.",
  n="The book is named 子罕 from its first words; Zhu Xi's heading counts thirty chapters (凡三十章); He Yan's counts thirty-one, and adds that Huang Kan's text has thirty. 罕 hǎn 'seldom' (Zhu Xi: 罕，少也; He Yan: 希也). 與 yǔ here is 'and', joining the three objects of 言 'to speak of'. 命 is what Heaven decrees — a man's lot and the order of things. Zhu Xi quotes Master Cheng: reckoning profit harms what is right; the principle of the decree is subtle; the Way of humaneness is great — all three are things the Master seldom spoke of. He Yan's commentary (unattributed): 利 is 'the harmony of what is right' (利者義之和也, a phrase from the Changes); 命 is Heaven's decree; 仁 is the fullness of conduct; few could reach these, so he seldom spoke of them. Readers have long been struck that humaneness is in fact discussed on page after page of the book; both our commentaries nonetheless let 罕 govern all three nouns, and so do we."),
# ---------------- 9.2 ----------------
dict(pas="9.2", w="達巷=dá xiàng/Daxiang — the name of a village district//to get through//a lane | 黨/village | 人/a man (of) | 曰：「/said | ^大/great | 哉/how | 孔子！=kǒng zǐ/Master Kong | ^博/broadly | 學/learned | 而/and yet | 無/(he) has nothing | 所/by which | 成/(he has) made | 名！」/a name",
  l="A man of the Daxiang village said: “How great is Master Kong!\nBroadly learned, and yet nothing by which he has made a name!”",
  i="A man from the village of Daxiang said: “What a great man Master Kong is! His learning is so wide, and yet there is no one thing he is known for.”",
  n="達巷 Daxiang is, Zhu Xi says, the name of the 黨 'village group' (He Yan's Zheng Xuan: five hundred households make a 黨); the man's own name, Zhu Xi adds, has not come down. 大哉 'great — how!': the predicate put first in an exclamation. 孔子 'Master Kong' is how outsiders speak of Confucius. 無所成名 'has nothing by which to make a name': 所 turns 成名 into 'that by which (he) makes a name'. Zhu Xi: he admired the breadth of the Master's learning and regretted that he had not made a name in any single art. Zheng Xuan says the same: broad in the Way and the arts, without making a name in any one."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 聞/heard | 之，/of it | ^謂/(and) said to | 門/(of his) gate, school | 弟子=dì zǐ/the disciples | 曰：「/saying | ^吾/I | 何/what | 執？/(shall) take up | ^執/take up | 御=yù/driving (a chariot) | 乎？/(question) | ^執/take up | 射=shè/archery | 乎？/(question) | ^吾/I | 執/(will) take up | 御=yù/driving | 矣。」/(so it is settled)",
  l="The Master heard of it and said to the disciples of his school:\n“What shall I take up?\nTake up driving?\nTake up archery?\nI will take up driving.”",
  i="When the Master heard of this, he said to his disciples: “What should I specialise in, then? Charioteering? Archery? I'll make it charioteering.”",
  n="門弟子 'the disciples of his gate', his school (as 8.3). 吾何執 'I — what shall I take up?': a question word object stands before the verb. 執 'to grasp', here, Zhu Xi says, 'to hold to one thing only' (專執): to specialise. 御 yù is driving a chariot, 射 shè archery — two of the six arts (He Yan's Zheng Xuan: he wished to be named for the lowliest of the six arts). 矣 settles it: 'I will take up driving, then.' Zhu Xi: archery and driving are each a single art, and driving, the work of a man's servant, is the lower of the two; the Master is saying, 'You would have me make a name by holding to something? Then I will hold to driving' — he answers praise with modesty. He quotes Yin Tun: the sage's Way is whole and his virtue complete, and he cannot be pointed to by any one strength; the man of Daxiang saw how great Confucius was and how broad his learning, and regretted that he had no single good thing to be known for — he admired the sage without understanding him."),
