dict(pas="9.3", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^麻=má/hemp, linen | 冕，/cap | 禮/(is) the rites | 也；/(assertion) | ^今/now | 也/(pause) | 純，=chún/silk | 儉；/(it is) economical | ^吾/I | 從=cóng/follow | 衆。」/the many",
  l="The Master said: “The linen cap — that is the rites;\nnowadays silk — it is economical;\nI follow the many.”",
  i="The Master said: “The rites call for a cap of linen; now people use silk, which costs less, and in this I go along with everyone else.”",
  n="麻冕 'a hemp cap': Zhu Xi identifies it as the black cloth cap (緇布冠). 純 chún here is 'silk' (Zhu Xi and He Yan's Kong Anguo: 純，絲也), not its usual 'pure'. 儉 'sparing, economical' — Zhu Xi: 省約. 今也 'now': 也 marks the pause after a time word. Zhu Xi explains the economy: the cloth cap was made of cloth of thirty 'sheng' 升, and a sheng is eighty threads, so the warp has two thousand four hundred threads — so fine and close that it is hard to make, and silk is cheaper. Kong Anguo: silk is easily made, so he follows the economy. The speech runs on past Zhu Xi's comment into the next unit, which is left unquoted."),
dict(w="拜=bài/bowing | 下，/below (the hall) | 禮/(is) the rites | 也；/(assertion) | ^今/now | 拜=bài/(they) bow | 乎/at | 上，/the top | 泰/arrogant | 也。/(it is) | ^雖/although | 違/(it) goes against | 衆，/the many | ^吾/I | 從=cóng/follow | 下。/(bowing) below",
  l="Bowing below — that is the rites;\nnowadays they bow on top — it is arrogant.\nThough it goes against the many,\nI follow ‘below’.",
  i="To bow at the foot of the steps is what the rites require; now people bow only once they are up in the hall, which is presumptuous. Here I keep to the foot of the steps, even if it means going against everyone else.",
  n="Zhu Xi: when a minister performs a rite with his lord he should bow at the foot of the hall; the lord declines the honour, and only then does the minister go up and complete the bow. 拜乎上 'bow at the top': 乎 is a preposition here, 'at' (= 於). 泰 'grand', here 'overbearing, arrogant' (Zhu Xi: 驕慢也). 雖 'although'. He Yan's Wang Su gives the same account: ministers of the day had grown arrogant and bowed above; to keep the bow below is respect. Zhu Xi quotes Master Cheng on the pair: where a custom does no harm to what is right, the gentleman may follow it; where it harms what is right, he may not."),
# ---------------- 9.4 ----------------
dict(pas="9.4", w="子/the Master | 絶=jué/was utterly without | 四：/four (things) | ^毋/no | 意，=yì/private notions | ^毋/no | 必，/insisting | ^毋/no | 固，/stubbornness | ^毋/no | 我。/self",
  l="The Master was utterly without four things:\nno private notions,\nno insisting,\nno stubbornness,\nno self.",
  i="Four things were wholly absent from the Master: he had no notions of his own to push, no insistence that things must go one way, no stubbornness, and no self to put first.",
  n="絶 jué (the edition's form of 絕) 'to cut off': Zhu Xi — to be without something entirely (無之盡者). 毋 is usually 'do not!', but here, Zhu Xi says, it stands for 無 'not having', and the Shiji, which writes 無, is right; he quotes Master Cheng: this 毋 is not a word of prohibition — the sage was without these four, so what need to forbid them? Zhu Xi's glosses: 意 yì is private intent (私意), 必 insisting in advance on a sure outcome (期必), 固 sticking fast (執滯), 我 the private self (私己). They form a cycle: they start in intent, are carried through by insisting, linger in stubbornness and are completed in the self; intent and insisting come before an affair, stubbornness and self after it, and the self breeds new intent in turn, so that desire for things drags one round without end. He quotes Zhang Zai: have even one of the four and one is unlike Heaven and Earth; and Yang Shi: only someone wise enough to know the sage, watching him closely and taking it in silently, could have recorded this. He Yan's commentary reads each as conduct: no 意, because he took the Way as his measure and did not follow his own will; no 必, because he acted when used and withdrew when set aside; no 固, because nothing was simply allowed or ruled out for him; no 我, because he handed on the old without making it himself and did not set himself apart from others."),
