dict(pas="9.5", w="子/the Master | 畏=wèi/was in danger, on his guard | 於/at | 匡。=kuāng/Kuang",
  l="The Master was in danger at Kuang.",
  i="The Master was surrounded and threatened at Kuang.",
  n="畏 wèi is usually 'to fear'; Zhu Xi: here it means to be on one's guard (有戒心之謂). 匡 Kuang is a place. Zhu Xi cites the Shiji: Yang Hu had once done violence at Kuang, and the Master looked like Yang Hu, so the men of Kuang surrounded him. He Yan's Bao Xian tells it more fully: Yan Ke, a disciple of the Master, had been with Yang Hu at that time, and later was driving the Master's chariot when they came to Kuang; the people recognised Ke, and the Master looked like Hu, so they surrounded him with weapons."),
dict(w="曰：「/(he) said | ^文王=wén wáng/King Wen//pattern, letters (his posthumous title)//king | 旣=jì/being already | 沒，=mò/gone, dead | ^文/letters | 不/(are they) not | 在/present | 兹=zī/here | 乎？」/(question)",
  l="He said: “King Wen being gone,\nare letters not here?”",
  i="He said: “King Wen is long dead — but is not the pattern he left, the heritage of letters, here, with me?”",
  n="文王 King Wen, father of King Wu, founder of Zhou. 旣 jì (the edition's form of 既) 'already'; 沒 mò 'to die'. 兹 zī 'this, here' (Zhu Xi and Kong Anguo: 此也) — Confucius means himself. 文 is 'letters, the literary arts' (our standing rendering), but Zhu Xi widens it here: the Way where it shows itself is called 文 — the rites, music, and the institutions; and he says the Master speaks of 文 rather than 'the Way' out of modesty. Kong Anguo: though King Wen is dead, his 文 is present here — 'here' is his own person. The speech runs on past Zhu Xi's comment into the next unit, which is left unquoted."),
dict(w="天/Heaven | 之/(clause marker) | 將=jiāng/were about to | 喪=sàng/destroy | 斯/this | 文/(heritage of) letters | 也，/(supposition) | ^後/later | 死/dying | 者/one who (is) | 不/would not | 得/get | 與=yù/to share | 於/in | 斯/this | 文/(heritage of) letters | 也；/(so it would be) | ^天/Heaven | 之/(clause marker) | 未/(has) not yet | 喪=sàng/destroyed | 斯/this | 文/(heritage of) letters | 也，/(since) | ^匡=kuāng/Kuang | 人/the men of | 其/then | 如=rú/do with | 予=yú/me | 何？/what",
  l="If Heaven were about to destroy this heritage of letters,\none who died later would not have got to share in it;\nsince Heaven has not yet destroyed this heritage of letters,\nthe men of Kuang — what then can they do about me?",
  i="If Heaven meant this heritage to perish, it would never have let one born after King Wen have a part in it. Since Heaven has not let it perish, how could the men of Kuang touch me?",
  n="The print has 子, a misprint for 予, corrected here. 予 yú 'me' is what the sense needs: Zhu Xi's comment paraphrases the clause as 匡人其奈我何 'what can the men of Kuang do to ME?'; both collation witnesses (Ruan's 1816 text and He Yan's text) read 予; and He Yan's Ma Rong says 如予何 is like saying 奈我何. 如 X 何 is the frame 'what (can one) do with X?' (as 7.22 如予何); 其 adds 'then, after all'. 喪 sàng 'to lose, let perish' (Zhu Xi: 喪與並去聲); 與 yù 'to take part in'. 之 between subject and verb makes 天之將喪斯文 a clause, 'Heaven's being about to destroy this 文', here a supposition, 'if'. 後死者 'the one who dies later' is Confucius himself, later than King Wen. Zhu Xi gives the argument in words he credits to 'Mr Ma' (馬氏) — words that in He Yan's commentary stand partly under Kong Anguo's name: King Wen being dead, Confucius calls himself 'the one who dies after'. If Heaven wished to destroy this 文, it would not have let me share in it; I have shared in it, so Heaven does not wish to destroy it; and if Heaven does not, then what can the men of Kuang do to me? — that is, they cannot go against Heaven to harm me. Compare 7.22, where the Master says the same of Huan Tui."),
# ---------------- 9.6 ----------------
dict(pas="9.6", w="大宰=tài zǎi/the Grand Steward//great (read tài: Zhu Xi 大音泰)//steward | 問/asked | 於/of | 子貢=zǐ gòng/Zigong | 曰：「/saying | ^夫子=fū zǐ/your Master | 聖=shèng/a sage | 者/one who is | 與？=yú/(is he?) | ^何/how | 其/he (is) | 多/many | 能/(in) abilities | 也？」/(question)",
  l="The Grand Steward asked Zigong:\n“Is your Master one who is a sage?\nHow is it he has so many abilities?”",
  i="The Grand Steward asked Zigong: “Your Master — is he a sage? How else could he be so able in so many things?”",
  n="大宰 tài zǎi 'Grand Steward' is a title (Zhu Xi: 大音泰, read tài); He Yan's text writes 太宰. Zhu Xi quotes Mr Kong (孔氏, Kong Anguo): an office; whether of Wu or of Song cannot be known. 子貢 Zigong is Duanmu Ci 端木賜 (1.10). 聖者與 'one who is a sage?': 與 yú (平聲) is the final particle of a doubtful question (Zhu Xi: 與者，疑辭). 何其多能也 'how is it he is so many-abled?' Zhu Xi: the Grand Steward probably took having many abilities to be what made a sage. He Yan's Kong Anguo puts the Steward's question differently: he was puzzled that Confucius was able in so many small skills (疑孔子多能於小藝)."),
dict(w="子貢=zǐ gòng/Zigong | 曰：「/said | ^固/surely | 天/Heaven | 縱=zòng/has given free rein | 之/to him | 將=jiāng/(to be) all but | 聖，=shèng/a sage | ^又/and besides | 多/many | 能/(his) abilities | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="Zigong said: “Surely Heaven has given him free rein to be all but a sage,\nand besides, he has many abilities.”",
  i="Zigong said: “Heaven has indeed set no bounds to him, and made him very nearly a sage; that he can do so many things comes on top of that.”",
  n="固 'surely, indeed'. 縱 zòng 'to let loose, give free rein' — Zhu Xi: like 肆, it means there is no limit or measure set to him. 將 jiāng here is 'nearly, all but' (Zhu Xi: 殆也) — a modest word, as though Zigong did not dare claim to know. 又 'besides': Zhu Xi — a sage penetrates everything, and many abilities are a by-product (餘事), so Zigong says 'besides' to add them on. He Yan's Kong Anguo reads 將聖 as 'great sage': Heaven has indeed given him the virtue of a great sage, and also made him able in many things."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 聞/heard | 之，/of it | 曰：「/(and) said | ^大宰=tài zǎi/the Grand Steward | 知=zhī/know | 我/me | 乎？/(does he) | ^吾/I | 少=shào/(when) young | 也/(pause) | 賤，/(was) of low station | ^故/so | 多/many | 能/(I) could do | 鄙/common | 事。/things | ^君子/the gentleman | 多/many (abilities) | 乎/(question) | 哉？/(emphatic) | ^不/not | 多/many | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master heard of it and said: “Does the Grand Steward know me?\nWhen I was young I was of low station,\nso I could do many common things.\nDoes the gentleman have many? He does not have many.”",
  i="When the Master heard of it, he said: “How much does the Grand Steward know of me? I was poor and of no account when I was young, and so I learned to turn my hand to a great many humble jobs. Does a gentleman need so many skills? He does not.”",
  n="少 shào 'young' (Zhu Xi marks it 去聲 at 9.9; here too it is 'young', not shǎo 'few'). 吾少也賤 'I, young — lowly': 也 marks the pause after the time phrase. 鄙事 'common, menial affairs'. 君子多乎哉 'the gentleman — many (abilities)?' with 乎哉 a strong rhetorical question, answered at once: 不多也. 大宰知我乎 can be heard as a question ('does he know me?') or an exclamation ('the Grand Steward knows me!' — a wry admission that he was indeed able in many things); He Yan's text reads 大宰知我者乎 'is the Grand Steward one who knows me?'. Zhu Xi: it was because he was young and lowly that he had many abilities, and what he could do were menial things — it was not that sageliness let him penetrate everything; and since many abilities are no way to lead others, he goes on to say the gentleman need not have them. He Yan's Bao Xian: when I was young and poor I always did things myself, so I could do a lowly man's work; the gentleman ought not to be many-abled."),
dict(w="牢=láo/Lao — the disciple Qin Lao | 曰：「/said | ^子/the Master | 云：『/said | ^吾/I | 不/was not | 試，=shì/employed | ^故/therefore | 藝。』」/(I became) skilled in the arts",
  l="Lao said: “The Master said:\n‘I was not employed,\ntherefore I am skilled in the arts.’”",
  i="Lao said: “The Master once told us: ‘It is because I was never given office that I picked up so many skills.’”",
  n="牢 Lao, Zhu Xi says, was a disciple of Confucius, surname Qin 琴, courtesy name Zikai 子開 or, by another account, Zizhang 子張; He Yan's Zheng Xuan calls him 'the disciple Zilao' (子牢). The text names him by his personal name, and we keep 'Lao'. 云 'to say', introducing a quoted saying. 試 shì 'to try out', here 'to be employed' (Zhu Xi and Zheng Xuan: 試，用也). 藝 yì 'skill, the arts', here used as a verb, 'became skilled'. Zhu Xi: because he was not used by the world, he was able to practise the arts and master them. He quotes Wu (吳氏): when the disciples recorded the Master's words above, Zilao recalled something he had heard before that was close in meaning, and so it was recorded with them — which is why it stands in this chapter."),
# ---------------- 9.7 ----------------
dict(pas="9.7", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^吾/I | 有/have | 知=zhī/knowledge | 乎/(question) | 哉？/(emphatic) | ^無/(I) have no | 知=zhī/knowledge | 也。/(assertion) | ^有/there was | 鄙/a common | 夫=fū/fellow | 問/(who) asked | 於/of | 我，/me | ^空空=kōng kōng/empty, blank//empty//empty | 如/(-like) | 也，/(pause) | ^我/I | 叩=kòu/knocked at | 其/its | 兩/two | 端/ends | 而/and | 竭/exhausted (it) | 焉。」/(in it)",
  l="The Master said: “Do I have knowledge?\nI have no knowledge.\nThere was a common fellow who asked of me,\nempty-like,\nand I knocked at its two ends and exhausted it.”",
  i="The Master said: “Am I a man who knows things? No, I know nothing. But when some simple fellow comes to me with a question, his head quite empty, I tap the matter at both ends, and I hold back nothing.”",
  n="知 zhī here is 'knowledge, knowing'. 鄙夫 'a common, rustic fellow' (夫 fū 'a man'). 空空如也 'empty-like': 空空 kōng kōng doubles 'empty' to describe, and 如 'like' (as 7.4 申申如也) makes it 'in an empty way'. Whose emptiness? He Yan's Kong Anguo says plainly the questioner's (其意空空然, 'his mind blank'), and Zhu Xi's 'even to the most foolish' agrees. 叩 kòu 'to knock' (Zhu Xi: 音口, kǒu — a reading modern practice does not keep; we give kòu), here 'to set going, stir up' (Zhu Xi: 發動也). 兩端 'the two ends' — Zhu Xi: like 'both heads': beginning and end, root and branch, high and low, fine and coarse, nothing left out. 竭焉 'used it all up': I hold nothing back. Zhu Xi: Confucius modestly says he has no knowledge, only that in teaching others, even the most foolish, he dares not do less than his utmost. He quotes Master Cheng: the sage's teaching stoops to meet people like this, and still fears they will find it lofty and keep their distance — the sage's Way must come down to them, while a worthy's words must be raised up, or the Way is not honoured; and Yin Tun: the sage's words cover high and low together — close enough that anyone can grasp them, and at their furthest so complete that even a sage could add nothing; this is what 'two ends' means, as in his answer to Fan Chi about humaneness and knowing (12.22). He Yan's own comment on 吾有知乎哉: one who 'knows' does not necessarily tell all he knows; I truly tell all."),
